{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "For single bracket: multiply outside term by each inside term",
   "For double brackets: multiply each term in the first bracket by each term in the second (FOIL)",
   "Collect like terms (usually the two middle terms combine)",
   "For squared brackets: use (a+b)² = a² + 2ab + b²"
  ],
  "title": "How to Expand Brackets",
  "content": "<p><strong>Single brackets:</strong> Multiply every term inside the bracket by the term outside. E.g. \\(3(2x + 5) = 6x + 15\\).</p><p><strong>Double brackets:</strong> Use FOIL (First, Outer, Inner, Last) or the grid method. Every term in the first bracket multiplies every term in the second. Then collect like terms.</p><p><strong>Squaring a bracket:</strong> \\((a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\\). Do NOT just square each term — the middle term \\(2ab\\) is essential.</p><p><strong>Triple brackets:</strong> Expand any two brackets first, then multiply the result by the third bracket. Collect like terms at each stage.</p><p><strong>Difference of two squares:</strong> \\((a + b)(a - b) = a^2 - b^2\\). The middle terms cancel.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Expand and simplify</strong> \\((x + 3)(x - 5)\\)</p><p><strong>F:</strong> \\(x \\times x = x^2\\)</p><p><strong>O:</strong> \\(x \\times (-5) = -5x\\)</p><p><strong>I:</strong> \\(3 \\times x = 3x\\)</p><p><strong>L:</strong> \\(3 \\times (-5) = -15\\)</p><p><strong>Collect:</strong> \\(x^2 - 5x + 3x - 15 = x^2 - 2x - 15\\)</p>"
 },
